WEBVTT

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:19.690
No matter where on earth you find yourself, clothing plays an important role in keeping you covered, healthy, and protected.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.440
Eskimos in the north bundle up to protect from harsh cold and blistering winds.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.970
If you live near a desert climate, you cover up not...

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.140
Not because it's cold, but because the sun rays are so powerful.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.840
Believe it or not, one of NASA's satellites has the same problems when dealing with its

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:39.240
environment.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.620
Just as clothes cover our skin and help protect us from nature's elements, the cold winter

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.660
wind and scorching summer sun, thermal blankets protect the Hubble satellite from the harsh

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:50.640
environment of space.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:56.180
You could say thermal blankets are to spacecraft what clothes are to people.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.920
But why would a satellite need clothes?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.200
Let's explore a little further.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.550
The Hubble satellite orbits Earth at 8 kilometers per second.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.010
That means that it fully circles the planet in 97 minutes.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.700
It completes about 15 of these circles, or orbits, each day.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.570
As the Hubble satellite travels through Earth's shadow,

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:18.990
then over to the side lit by the sun and around to the shadow again,

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.550
the telescope is exposed to both the extreme cold of deep space

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:26.450
and the powerful heat of the sun in a fast cycle over and over.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.980
The thermal blanket's outer layer changes...

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.680
about 118 degrees celsius that's about 245 degrees fahrenheit every 45 minutes so the blanket must be

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.270
able to insulate and protect hubble's equipment from such extreme temperatures so how does the

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:49.240
hubble blanket do this the blanketing material used on the telescope is made up of 16 layers

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:54.950
of dimpled aluminum with an outer teflon skin the dimples make the aluminum look like this crumpled

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:59.850
piece of aluminum foil see the difference the dimples give the metal room to contract or get

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.860
smaller with the cold and expand or get bigger with the heat without cracking the metal.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:08.080
Teflon is highly temperature resistant and is a terrific insulator.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.520
That means it doesn't burn up in high temperatures and maintains a consistent temperature on

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:12.960
the inside.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:18.020
The thermal blanket does a good job of protecting the onboard instruments against extreme temperature

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:18.440
swings.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:24.030
Can you believe it does all that even though it's less than three millimeters thick when

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:24.510
laid flat?

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.310
That's about this thin, amazing.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.300
It's an impressive blanket, but nothing lasts forever.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:36.390
The space environment is hard on materials, so the blanket does need to be repaired from

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:36.970
time to time.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:40.460
The wear and tear on the outer layer begins on day one of its orbit.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:49.600
Starting with the second servicing mission in December of 1997, we noticed that the outer

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.560
layer of the thermal blankets were degrading and cracking at a very fast rate.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.800
But instead of taking the precious astronaut time to remove and replace the thermal blankets,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:02:59.980
we decided to

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.630
a different approach. We covered them with what we call new outer blanket layers or nobles.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.730
They're large stainless steel panels that provide the same layer of protection as thermal blankets

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:16.730
do. It's just a different way of thermally protecting a spacecraft. Think about it. It's

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:20.350
kind of like when you wear a hole in your favorite pair of jeans and then your mom has to put a patch

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:26.680
on it. The Hubble has been in space since 1990. That's quite a while for Hubble thermal blanket

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.920
duty. And over time, the outer Teflon layers started to crash.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:36.200
It's very important to repair and replace the blanket so the Hubble telescope that rides on the satellite can still continue to work,

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.680
sending great pictures of the universe back to Earth.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:44.580
The NASA Goddard Space Flight Center in Greenbelt, Maryland houses just the place for such needed repairs.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:52.330
There, a group of upholsters, costume designers, materials experts, and mechanical engineers make up a team of thermal blanket experts.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:58.430
With these types of experts around, this NASA center is the perfect home for the designing and making of the blankets.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.940
It's also where the old blank

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.000
astronauts have brought back on previous repair missions can be studied.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.970
Since the Hubble telescope was launched into orbit,

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.810
there have already been a few repair missions.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.220
The knowledge gained from the thermal blankets

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:11.920
that were returned to Earth from those missions

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.000
helped scientists and engineers develop better blankets for the future.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.790
And that new knowledge isn't just for Hubble,

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.360
it's for a bunch of other future space-based missions too.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.620
Sharing information and data is just one of the ways

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.620
engineers at Goddard are working to make sure

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.000
future spacecraft last a long time in space.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:34.030
In other words, the work being done at NASA will help the Hubble, as well as other missions,

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.990
keep doing what they do best, explore the universe.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.140
And by the way, don't forget to wear the right clothing when you're exploring our world too.
